Timestamp the heartbeat from the boot clock

clock_get_system_microtime is the system clock: it is seeded from the device's
real-time clock and adjusted, so its zero is not the boot.  The panel showed
183.29 seconds on a boot that had been running for well under a minute,
which says nothing about elapsed time.  The boot clock
(clock_get_boottime_microtime) counts from the boot and never moves
backwards, which is the time the watchdog timeout and the matching
intervals have to be read against.
---
diff --git a/osfmk/kern/startup.c b/osfmk/kern/startup.c
index 699ddc0..98f885b 100644
--- a/osfmk/kern/startup.c
+++ b/osfmk/kern/startup.c
@@ -401,12 +401,12 @@ nio_heartbeat_thread(void *arg, wait_result_t wr)
 	uint32_t nio_hb_last = 0;
 	for (;;) {
 		clock_sec_t nio_hb_sec;
-		clock_usec_t nio_hb_usec;
+		clock_nsec_t nio_hb_usec;
 		uint32_t nio_hb_now = 0;
 
 		delay(100);
 		n++;
-		clock_get_system_microtime(&nio_hb_sec, &nio_hb_usec);
+		clock_get_boottime_microtime(&nio_hb_sec, &nio_hb_usec);
 		nio_hb_now = nio_hb_usec + (uint32_t)(nio_hb_sec % 1000) * 1000000u;
 		if ((uint32_t)(nio_hb_now - nio_hb_last) >= 1000000u) {
 			nio_hb_last = nio_hb_now;
